      "rev_n_8_3c7852_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_3c7852_1",
        "node_id": "n_8_3c7852",
        "thought_text": "Thought-2: Instead of always adding the cheapest available item, consider a different approach. Since we need to avoid forming pairs that sum up to more than X, we can use a two-pointer technique after sorting the prices in ascending order. Initialize two pointers, one at the beginning (left) and one at the end (right) of the sorted list. If the sum of the prices at these two pointers is less than or equal to X, move both pointers inward, indicating that both items can participate in the offer. If the sum exceeds X, move only the right pointer inward, as moving the left pointer would increase the sum further. Continue this process until the pointers meet or cross each other. The total number of items between the two pointers (exclusive) will be the maximum number of items that can participate in the offer without violating the condition.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "636808690586209895",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_8_3c7852_0",
